{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785260916,"nonce":"ibb/QqvDMlM6MV/XKatlZp5Z2KU=","title":"ADR-0064 s1: warm-first write with one batched cold flush — supersede TieredCas's per-blob cold-first ordering","message":"`TieredCas::put_blob`/`put_tree` `await` **cold first** per object and propagate its error; `TieredCas::ingest` then walks the tree a **second** time for warm. So a drain pays a network round-trip **per file** on the critical path — the 4–6 ms/file ADR-0061 measured as **81–88%** of the cost it set out to remove — plus a duplicate walk.\n\nADR-0064 replaces this: write warm locally in **one walk**, then **one batched flush** to cold, with the flush synchronous with respect to `Succeeded`. Strictly faster; the part 4 guarantee is unchanged.\n\n**The comment at `crates/scarab-storage/src/tiered.rs:250` (\"Cold first: this is the leg that licenses `Succeeded`\") must be rewritten, not deleted** — it is currently the clearest statement of the old mechanism and a reader who finds it will trust it.\n\nNote the ADR-0062 s4 code already shipped against the OLD ordering: `crates/scarab-server/src/workspaced.rs`'s `reingest_cold_then_warm` deliberately mirrors `TieredCas::ingest`. It has to move with this.\n\n- [ ] Warm-first, single walk, one batched flush; flush gates the settle response\n- [ ] A partial flush never reports success; the batch is idempotent (content-addressed keys will be re-offered on retry)\n- [ ] The two-walk `ingest` goes, and with it the root-disagreement tripwire between two independent walks — the `put_tree` canonicalisation-skew tripwire is a DIFFERENT hazard and stays\n- [ ] A flush failure fails the Attempt and names the cause (never a mystery I/O error)\n- [ ] `tiered.rs:250`'s comment rewritten to describe the new mechanism\n- [ ] A test proving the flush is on the critical path: kill cold, assert the settle does not report durable","files":null}]}